      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Jorge, thanks for starting this. A map of the tree is useful for people and for agents. I checked all 26 files and checked the paths, symbols, targets and flags against the code at PS2. The problem is that a README for agents has to be exact: an agent trusts what it reads and builds on it. Right now about twenty claims do not match the code. I left them as inline comments.\n\nA few general suggestions:\n\n1. Skia already has docs for agents in agents/ (core_skills.json, skills/skia-gn-workflow, skills/skia-add-test). The new READMEs do not mention them and sometimes say the opposite (see the ninja --quiet comments). Please link to the skills and to site/docs for build and test steps instead of copying them. With three copies, they will drift apart within weeks.\n\n2. Keep only what changes rarely: what the directory is for, where its boundaries are, the main types and entry points, and how it relates to other directories. Line anchors (SkShaper.h#L197-L248), full lists of files, classes and tests, and counts like \"14 modular libraries\" go stale with every unrelated change, and nothing will catch it.\n\n3. Please split the CL by area (core, gpu, sksl, pdf, modules, build/test) and add each area\u0027s OWNERS as reviewers. About 1350 lines of docs across the whole tree in one CL is too much to review well, and the people who can catch errors in src/gpu/ are not the same people who can catch them in src/pdf/.\n\n4. Before upload, run a simple check that every path, symbol and target in the .md files exists in the tree (a git grep per identifier is enough). It would have caught most of the inline comments.\n\nThe text-related parts (skparagraph, skshaper, src/text) are mostly right, so the approach works when each file gets a careful check against the code. I\u0027m happy to review those directories once they are split out.\n\nAssisted-by: AI",
